    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "Established on the `cas-certificate` route, in the geometry family. The condition set is minimal ABSOLUTELY, not merely relative to the search budget: `certify` tries the empty subset first and it is DECIDED -- 0.7-0.9 s, nonzero remainder -- so the theorem is genuinely not unconditional, and no larger budget, different monomial order or better algorithm could report it as such. (Ideal membership does not depend on the monomial order; only whether a verdict is reached inside the ceilings does. That is what turns a decided subset into an absolute statement, and it is the reason the order was safe to change under this theorem as well as the six older ones.) (`geometry_order_audit` establishes the same for all six older facts, where it was previously only budget-scoped.) This theorem differs from `F:geometry-parallelogram-diagonals-bisect` by exactly one hypothesis, the quadratic |AB| = |BC|, and uses the same non-degeneracy condition; that one generator is the difference between a 72 ms reduction and a 21 s one, and under the previous default monomial order it was the difference between a proof and a decline. `geometry.nondegeneracy.abd-not-collinear` is listed in the footprint even though it also appears as a hypothesis of `formal.statement`, deliberately: on this route the footprint is the field a reader scans to see what a proof rests on, and a side condition invisible there is precisely how a mechanised geometry proof comes to be quoted as unconditional.",
